      "data": {
        "grounded": true,
        "answer": "A commit in Git is a snapshot of the current state of the files in the repository. It has a unique ID and is linked to its parent, allowing you to travel back in time to a previous version of the files. [1] When you create a commit, you're taking a snapshot of the current state of the files, and the commit has a unique ID. [1] You can make multiple commits, and each one has its own unique ID, linked to its parent. [1]\n\nThe course covers the basics of commits, but it doesn't go into advanced topics like how to create a commit with a specific message or how to revert a commit.",
